\par\par \b \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  Stresses Hedging Plan Isn't  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2023\par 15:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0950 ET - In a surprise move, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  said it is considering hedging part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to reduce financial risks, but governor Erik Thedeen repeatedly stated in Thursday's press conference that the measure was not to be seen as \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 SEB\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief strategist Olle Holmgren says in a note. "Considering that the measure was presented at the same time as the rate decision which states that a weaker SEK is the main upside risk for inflation...it seems probable that the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  hopes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging will support the krona." EUR/SEK dropped back after the initial run higher this morning, but has headed higher again since Thedeen's comments, \uc2 SEB\uc2  says. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0942 ET - The rise in Germany's services inflation--to 5.3% in June from 4.5% in May--could spook some \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers, but they needn't worry, Pantheon Macroeconomics' chief eurozone economist \uc2 Claus Vistesen\uc2  says in a note. Base effects in transport services--owing to the anniversary of government rail-fare subsidies--have been well flagged, he says. Indeed, the separate regional CPIs clearly suggest that the jump in services inflation was driven by a jump in the transport services component, Vistesen says. Core inflation will remain elevated due to base effects in transport until August, but then there will likely be a big drop in September as underlying disinflation in goods and services hit the data more meaningfully, he adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 ET - Yield has returned to higher-quality, liquid segments of European fixed-income markets through a combination of higher government bond yields and wider credit spreads, Allspring's fixed-income team says. "We think this presents an opportunity for European and global bond investors to return to euro-denominated credit after many years of sourcing yield globally," Henrietta Pacquement, senior portfolio manager and head of global fixed income and Martijn de Vree, head of fixed income solutions in global fixed income, say. The size of European credit markets offers significant diversification opportunities as well, they say, adding that European investment-grade debt yields about 4%, and yields rise close to 6% when hedged back to the U.S. dollar. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 ET - Dockworkers at Canada's western ports of Vancouver and Prince Rupert warn of a three-day strike action starting Saturday over a salary increase impasse with employers. Unions and the \uc2 British Columbia Maritime Employers Association\uc2  have been trying to reach a new annual contract since the last contract expired in March. The strike will mostly affect boxships as tankers and other vessels will be allowed to dock. Vancouver and Prince Rupert are Canada's largest and third largest box ports, respectively, and also serve as big gateways for US cargo. (costas.paris@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0933 ET - German inflation figures for June, which were stronger than expected, won't change the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hawkish resolve in its tightening cycle, Franziska Palmas, senior Europe economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , says in a note. The CPI increased mainly on base effects, with core inflation ticking up entirely due to a jump in services inflation to 5.3% from 4.5%, reflecting the anniversary of the introduction of the EUR9 transport ticket, she says. But even if core inflation edges down in other countries, \uc2 ECB\uc2  officials are concerned that the tight labor market will slow the return of the core to target, Palmas says. "Only substantial and repeated falls in core and particularly services inflation would ease those worries," she adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 ET - The US median home listing price slipped 0.9% annually in June, posting the first yearly decline since 2017, Realtor.com says. At the same time, while home shoppers had more homes to choose from this month, improvement stalled as the active inventory growth rate slowed for the fourth month in a row, 7.1%, and came in well below May's 21.5% rate. Price gains have been weakening since last summer as rising mortgage rates have added to ongoing affordability challenges and further cooled buyer demand. Realtor.com says its revised 2023 outlook expects only a modest drop in home prices of 0.6% for the year. (chris.wack@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 ET - Higher yields have boosted returns in many bond portfolios year to date, helping investors weather economic uncertainties that continue to unfold around the world, Allspring's fixed income team says in its mid-year outlook. Inflation remains high in both Europe and the U.K., and core inflation in the U.S. remains sticky, it says, adding that this is keeping central banks in inflation-fighting mode. "However, an inflection point may have been reached in the first half of 2023, when central banks had to begin dealing with challenges beyond fighting inflation as global growth slowed and cracks appeared in the financial system," it says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0927 ET - The Swedish krona falls to a record low against the euro, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 , extending losses after the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  earlier hiked interest rates by 25 basis points to 3.75%, when some had anticipated a bigger 50bp increase. The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  faces similar challenges to other central banks but they are "more extreme," leaving substantial uncertainty about the future path of monetary policy, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  global economist \uc2 James Pomeroy\uc2  says in a note. "Inflation is still well-above its target, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains weak, and the economy is hardly growing in aggregate, with consumer spending falling sharply in recent quarters." \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects data will justify a pause in rate rises in September. EUR/SEK hits a high of 11.8437, \uc2 FactSet\uc2  data show. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 ET - The tone of the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly inflation report was consistent with the start of a cycle of rate cuts, probably as soon as the next monetary policy meeting in August, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Alberto Ramos in a research note. The bank also raised its forecast for economic growth this year to 2.0% from 1.2%, boosted by the improved outlook for industry, especially mining and utilities, and for services, Ramos says. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 ET - The rise in German inflation in June should only be a temporary pause before consumer prices ease once again, ING's global head of macro \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  says in a note. The rebound to 6.4%, from 6.1% in May, marks end of the disinflationary trend seen over the last six months, but there is evidence the trend could gain stronger momentum after the summer, he says. On-month food prices dropped for the third consecutive month, while clothing prices fell for the first time since January--a tentative sign of weaker demand and price discounts, Brzeski says. "With still lower-than-expected energy prices, dropping food prices and fading pipeline price pressures in both services and manufacturing, German inflation could come down faster than the \uc2 ECB\uc2  expects," he adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 ET - Today's fall in weekly jobless claims "is more likely noise than signal," Pantheon's \uc2 Ian Shepherdson\uc2  says in a note about today's data showing a decline to 239K from 265K. "We have no specific explanation for this sudden dip, after three straight weeks in a narrow range from 262K to 265K, but observations for individual weeks always have to be viewed with skepticism, especially when they move against the established trend in claims and other data," Shepherdson says. He adds that, considering seasonal factors, "a modest net dip in claims over the next few weeks is a reasonable bet, even as the underlying trend continues to rise." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0910 ET - Consumer spending grew at a 4.2% annual rate in 1Q, the fastest pace since mid-2021, when the economy was rebounding from pandemic lockdowns. Stronger consumer spending was a key driver behind the upward revision to 1Q GDP growth. Consumers splashed out on long-lasting goods in the first three months of the year, despite higher interest rates. Consumer spending on durables rose at a 16.3% annual rate. Their spending on services, which includes dining out and traveling, also notched a solid annual gain of 3.2% in 1Q. (sarah.chaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2023 09:50 ET (13:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230629ej6t00254\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
