\par\par \b \uc2 In Global Fight Over  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , Sweden Weighs Move\b0\par\par\uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2016\par 08:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 2/5/16) \par    By Charles Duxbury \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKHOLM -- A year after cutting its main interest rate below zero percent for the first time ever, the board of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sweden's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is considering further bold moves to keep a buoyant national \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in check and jolt desperately moribund inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prepares to announce policy decisions on Feb. 11, even lower interest rates, an extension of a bond-buying program and direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are on the table, said officials at \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Riksbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have become seasoned," long-serving Riksbank Deputy Gov. Per Jansson said in an interview. "Things which once made us say 'oh my God' don't seem that dramatic anymore."\par \par \uc2 Sweden is a small-scale but stark illustration of how many central banks, with a mandate to help tiny but steady inflation percolate through their economies, are wrestling with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the inflow of cheap commodity and energy pushing down price indexes, some central banks have become focused on taking inflation from one another by lowering the value of their national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes goods coming intothe country more expensive, raising domestic prices.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  seized the initiative a year ago when it cut its main rate to minus-0.1%. Since then, its advantage has been checked by sub-zero rate moves by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, as recently as last week, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Robert Bergqvist, an economist at Stockholm-based Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken AB and a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, is concerned this tit-for-tat approach to monetary policy won't be effective. He fears it can at best deliver short-term benefits for growth and inflation but little to address broader challenges, like high public debt or an aging population, which might be driving price growth lower globally.\par \par \uc2 "This kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war will not benefit anyone," Mr. Bergqvist said.\par \par \uc2 Central banks have used rate setting to guide their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a desired level in the past. This is the first time so many countries are burrowing through the underlands of monetary policy. In addition to Sweden, the eurozone and Japan, Denmark and Switzerland also have adopted negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Sweden now boasts a benchmark interest rate of minus-0.35% and a deposit rate of minus-1.1%, meaning that the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  is effectively charging banks willing to hold certain types of reserves with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The policy is designed to encourage banks to lend instead of sitting on cash and to dissuade foreign investors from holding a nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The trouble is Sweden's economy is growing at a healthy pace of about 3% a year and is in no particular need of the stimulus provided by negative rates.\par \par \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  policy makers said they are aware that housing prices have risen close to 20% in some parts of Sweden over the past year and that their ultra-accommodating policy risks causing bubbles in real estate and other assets. But if they do too little, expected further easing from \uc2 the ECB\uc2  next month could send the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring against the euro. "There is scope to do more," Mr. Jansson said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  found itself at a similar juncture last year. The first move below a zero-percent rate had provided a brief respite. After a few weeks, however, the krona was again rising against the euro, endangering Sweden's exporters by making their goods pricier, while cheaper imports risked sapping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to rekindle inflation.\par \par \uc2 Between mid-February and mid-March a fall in the euro from 9.60 Swedish kronor to 9.10 had Mr. Jansson and his colleagues scrambling to react.\par \par \uc2 On March 18, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  rushed into an unscheduled policy conclave and decided to go deeper into negative territory, cutting its main rate to minus-0.25% from minus-0.15%.\par \par \uc2 "It was clear that if that appreciation had continued, we would have been in big trouble," Mr. Jansson said.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the bank has sanctioned another rate cut and set a target to buy as much as 35% of Sweden's government debt from commercial banks. That is done with newly minted money, again to stimulate the economy by boosting lending.\par \par \uc2 The inflation target is a cornerstone of the small open Swedish economy, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  said. Introduced in New Zealand in 1990, the strategy guides wage setting and helps growth by dissuading citizens from saving in the hope of cheaper prices in the future.\par \par \uc2 Despite its expansive policy, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's inflation score card has disappointed and it hasn't hit its 2% target for four years. A year ago, the bank said inflation would hit the target this year, but by year-end its 2016 forecast had been cut to 1.3%. The annual inflation rate in December was 0.1%. To regain leverage, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  said late last year it was considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market directly, something it hasn't done in a decade and a half. The policy would involve selling kronor to push down their value.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 4, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  board held an unscheduled meeting and said it was in a position to act anytime. "The upturn in inflation still depends to a large extent on the weak krona," the board said after the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This article was corrected February 7, 2016 at 0153 GMT to show the correct spelling of Charles Duxbury's last name. The original version of this article incorrectly spelled it Duxbry.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 05, 2016 02:41 ET (07:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160205ec250011m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
