\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Struggling to raise inflation,  \highlight22\uc2 Sweden \highlight\uc2  edges towards  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2016\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Friday item)\par \par \uc2 * Crown strengthening on strong domestic economy, loose \uc2 ECB\uc2  policy\par \par \uc2 * Board gave governor power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week\par \par \uc2 * Rate setters split on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision\par \par \uc2 By Daniel Dickson and Oskar von Bahr\par \par \uc2 STOCKHOLM, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is close to making its first \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 15 years in a desperate attempt to push up inflation, but critics say any effort to weaken the crown is likely to be futile.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  has struggled to combat a largely strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite cutting interest rates to record negative lows, due to a booming economy and loose monetary policy in much of the outside world.\par \par \uc2 With its monetary tools to ward off deflation dwindling, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  board gave Governor Stefan Ingves powers earlier this week to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Minutes published on Friday of a meeting last month suggested that some policymakers believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the right way to go.\par \par \uc2 "Several members ... noted that if the exchange rate appreciated too rapidly, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be necessary," said the minutes of the rate-setting meeting held in mid-December.\par \par \uc2 The ultra-low interest rates helped the economy to grow 3.9 percent year-on-year in the third quarter, the third highest rate in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Data on Friday showed industrial production was up 6 percent in November from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 But with the main lending rate at -0.35 percent and the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  pumping money into the economy by buying bonds, it faces a quandary. The loose monetary policy is failing to raise inflation but encouraging a rise in house prices and household debt, which per capita is one of the highest in Europe. Many people also fear a housing bubble.\par \par \uc2 "The tool box is getting empty," said Nordea analyst \uc2 Torbjorn Isaksson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That leaves \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the hope of pushing inflation towards the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's target by raising the cost of imported goods and services. Analysts believe the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  could start selling crowns at somewhere between 9.00 to 9.15 to the euro versus 9.26 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 TARGET OBSESSION\par \par \uc2 Critics say the bank's obsession with its 2 percent target is unrealistic. But the board does not dare to wait for the strong domestic economy to push up inflation, wary of past policy mistakes that undermined its credibility.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  raised rates fast in 2010 and 2011, worried about the housing bubble that was already inflating, even though the global economy had yet to recover from the 2008 financial crisis. Nobel Laureate Paul Krugman called the policy "sadomonetarist" while U.S. Federal Reserve chief \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  held up premature Swedish rate increases as a mistake to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Former Bank of England governor Mervyn King and U.S. economist Professor Marvin Goodfriend are due this month to present a post mortem report on the last few years of Swedish monetary policy, including the viability of the inflation target.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices have been flat or falling for most of the last three years, and a meagre pick up toward the end of last year has stalled, largely due to the stronger crown. CPI rose by 0.1 pct in the year to November.\par \par \uc2 FIGHTING AGAINST GRAVITY\par \par \uc2 Many people think that if the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  resorts to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will be unable to beat the market. "The crown is in our opinion - and according to the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  - undervalued so they are fighting against gravity," \uc2 Nordea\uc2 's Isaksson said.\par \par \uc2 The problems with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were highlighted only last year when the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to scrap its cap on the Swiss franc versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of a split within the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 , Deputy Governor Martin Floden entered a reservation against the board's decision to give Ingves the powers to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately.\par \par \uc2 On its trade-weighted index, the crown has strengthened 5.8 percent since April and is 2.9 percent stronger since the day before the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  cut rates at an unscheduled meeting in March.\par \par \uc2 Threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have caused the crown to weaken from its strongest levels in December, but the index is still stronger than the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's latest forecast, at 109.06 versus 110.7 seen around now.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll on Thursday showed that despite the prospects of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market strategists expect the crown to reach 9.00 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Many believe \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be backed by other policy measures such as further rate cuts and more bond buying.\par \par \uc2 In Friday's minutes, Floden advocated further rate cuts. However, critics says they cannot be cut infinitely below zero and market participants are already complaining of low liquidity even though the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  has yet to complete purchases of more than 30 percent of outstanding government bonds.\par \par \uc2 The last time the \uc2 Riksbank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was in 2001. At that time, the problem was the opposite: a weakening Swedish crown meant there was a risk of higher inflation than the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's 2 percent target.\par \par \uc2 A 2006 study published by the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  itself showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been of little value, also after a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg that the bank upheld at tremendous costs, including a 500 percent policy rate, until 1992, was scrapped.\par \par \uc2 "That the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  alone could resist the whole of the market's flows or its will to affect the crown, I just don't believe in that," said Anders Skoldberg, portfolio manager at the \uc2 Second Swedish National Pension Fund\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Simon Johnson; Editing by Alistair Scrutton and David Stamp) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-11T09:09:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160111ec1b00acd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
