\par\par \b \uc2 Negative rates merited, but  \highlight22\uc2 Sweden \highlight\uc2  should avoid  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 -IMF\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2015\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 STOCKHOLM, Dec 2 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been right to adopt ultra-loose monetary policy but should not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push up prices unless absolutely necessary, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said on Wednesday.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Despite growth that is outpacing most of the developed world, benchmark rates in Sweden stand at a record low of minus 0.35 percent to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boost inflation.\par \par \par \par \uc2 In its annual report on Sweden, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  backed the negative rates and "supported the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's readiness to do more, if needed, until core inflation is durably close to target".\par \par \par \par \uc2 But it said the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  should not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, "except as a last resort given Sweden's strong external position".\par \par \par \par \uc2 The country has been running a large current account surplus for decades and a devaluation of the crown would make exporters even more competitive.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Negative rates have started to push up consumer prices with  underlying inflation at 1.1 percent on an annual basis in October, but the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  has said it will act again if the upturn is threatened.\par \par \par \par \uc2 With little room to cut rates further or expand its bond purchases, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it could consider \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to weaken the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and push up import prices.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The country faces further challenges in addition to low inflation, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 As many as 190,000 asylum seekers are expected to arrive this year and while many will have their claims rejected, the flood is straining short-term resources and will add to long term problems in the labour market, housing and the education system.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  called on authorities to adopt a range of measures, including phasing out mortgage interest tax breaks, to cool down a red-hot housing market. Rent controls should be abolished and reform is needed to boost competition in construction, it said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Sweden's authorities have been growing increasingly concerned a housing bubble is building, with prices soaring and mortgage debt levels among the highest in Europe.\par \par \par \par \uc2 But measures to address the problems have been delayed.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said it expected Sweden's economy to grow around 3 percent both this year and next. Growth in the euro zone is expected to be around 1.5 percent this year, according to the \uc2 OECD\uc2 .\par \par \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Simon Johnson; editing by Niklas Pollard)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-5T14:00:01.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151202ebc200ho5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
