\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Sweden risks property bubble as  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war intensifies\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats from Sunday, without changes)\par \par \uc2 * Riksbank steps up efforts to avoid deflation\par \par \uc2 * Strengthening crown seen as threat\par \par \uc2 * Monetary loosening could stoke consumer debt\par \par \uc2 * Key interest rate at -0.25 pct\par \par \uc2 By Johan Ahlander and Simon Johnson\par \par \uc2 STOCKHOLM, March 22 (Reuters) - Having twice cut rates into negative territory, \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is locked in a high-stakes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war with the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could stave off deflation but risks creating another property bubble.\par \par \uc2 The Riksbank is worried that a strengthening crown, fuelled in part by ECB bond-buying, will reverse a recent upturn in consumer prices and raise the spectre of deflationary damage to Sweden's fast-growing AAA economy.\par \par \uc2 The moves by Riksbank Governor Stefan Ingves represent a dramatic rethink by a bank that was accused of "sado monetarism" for keeping policy tight after the financial crisis for fear of stoking household debt that is among the highest in Europe at 170 percent of disposable income.\par \par \uc2 The Riksbank cut its key interest rate by 0.15 percentage points last week, to -0.25 percent, and said it would buy a further 30 billion Swedish crowns ($3.5 billion) of government bonds.\par \par \uc2 The danger, however, is that the ultra-loose monetary policy could encourage households to take on more debt.\par \par \uc2 "Our fundamental view is that they have been forced to take this aggressive action because of past mistakes," said James McCann, European economist at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The risk for them is if they go big on asset purchases it could feed through into bigger credit creation in the housing sector and they would be stoking financial stability risk."\par \par \uc2 CRIPPLING CRASH\par \par \uc2 Memories of Sweden's crippling property crash of the early 1990s remain vivid. Though set against a different backdrop -- high inflation was then endemic in Sweden -- a real estate lending boom crippled several banks as loans went bad, necessitating a costly state rescue.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the decade was spent slowly restocking public finances amid soaring unemployment that even now has yet to return to pre-crisis levels.\par \par \uc2 Making the Riksbank sweat now is a fall in long-term inflation expectations before wage negotiations for about a third of the country's population are finalised early next year. The deflationary scenario could become self-fulfilling if unions and bosses agree meagre salary increases.\par \par \uc2 Former finance minister \uc2 Anders Borg\uc2  said the Riksbank's slow reaction on low inflation meant it could ill afford a strong crown. Combined with low interest rates, fast economic growth threatens to create asset bubbles, especially in property.\par \par \uc2 "We must see some of the history coming here. Sweden has now had falling inflation for the past four years ... so it is quite clear we overestimated the inflationary pressures," Borg said, serving to explain why Sweden is taking a looser line than Britain, another European economy with good growth but deflation risks.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION OF TRUST\par \par \uc2 Par Magnusson, chief analyst at Nordics RBS, said: "Now inflation expectations are falling, so they are forced to go all in. How do you solve that? By giving a promise to the export industry that you will be weakening the crown ... The message is aimed at the employers: trust us and raise the wages and we will promise to weaken the crown."\par \par \uc2 The Riksbank's strategy may be working. The crown on Friday traded at around 9.32 to the euro, compared with 9.17 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action..\par \par \uc2 But other small countries, such as Switzerland, have had trouble winning a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war against the euro, with the ECB pledging to buy 60 billion euros ($64.9 billion) of bonds each month.\par \par \uc2 "It will be an extremely difficult task," said Robert Bergqvist, chief economist at SEB. "It's a very uneven battle between the ECB and the Riksbank."\par \par \uc2 If rate cuts fail, \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2  has other tools at its disposal, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or increased bond buying. Many traders expect more action if the crown strengthens again.\par \par \uc2 "I think they can win the war if they increase the size of the asset purchases," said Petr Krpata, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING in London. ($1 = 8.6210 Swedish crowns) ($1 = 0.9242 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Johan Sennero; Writing by Alistair Scrutton; Editing by David Goodman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-23T10:23:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150323eb3n0098c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
