\par\par \b \uc2 Swedish cbank has not ruled out  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\uc2 -Skingsley\b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 STOCKHOLM, March 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a possible measure to push up inflation, Deputy Governor Cecilia Skingsley said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "We can make further rate cuts, further bond purchases and also on the list is the possibility of lending to businesses and we have not excluded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  either" Skingsley told reporters on the sidelines of a conference.\par \par \uc2 She would not give a ranking of the measures available.\par \par \uc2 Skingsley said a recent pick-up in inflation figures showed that expansive policy was working but that inflation was still too low and that meant that "we are still on high alert to take further measures if we don't think the development is sufficiently favourable".\par \par \uc2 In February, the Riksbank cut its key interest rate to -0.10 percent and launched a bond buying program to push up prices and restore faith in its 2 percent inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The Riksbank also said it was ready to act on very short notice with further measures if needed.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Daniel Dickson and Simon Johnson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-17T09:38:56.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150317eb3h008g8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
