\par\par \b \uc2 With  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war threatening, speculators focus on Swedish crown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2015\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Speculators are turning their attention to the Swedish crown, expecting it to weaken as Sweden reacts to the threat of a widening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank might open fire as early as next week by cutting interest rates to less than zero and setting the stage for quantitative easing. But if those efforts fall short, analysts say, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to weaken the crown.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1.1 trillion-euro quantitative easing programme, announced last month, pushed the euro lower against a host of European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In response, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  abandoned its cap on the value of the franc and \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in record amounts.\par \par \uc2 "The Riksbank should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and talk the crown down like the Swiss and the Danes. That will have a much bigger impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than quantitative easing or rate cuts," said Carl Hammer, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SEB, a leading Nordic bank.\par \par \uc2 However, the Riksbank has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for more than a decade, so it is unlikely to do so immediately.\par \par \uc2 In the options market, investors are betting on a sustained period of crown weakness. One-month euro/Swedish crown risk reversals -- a gauge of demand for options on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising or falling -- show an increasing bias for crown weakness against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The pricing partly reflects expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin a QE programnme of its own. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  asset purchases usually weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by increasing its supply, but the Riksbank is expected to buy only 15 billion to 20 billion crowns (232.75 million euros) a month.\par \par \uc2 That will be dwarfed by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 60 billion euros a month, so it is unlikely to be a game changer. [ID:nL6N0V52J6\} Indeed, a Reuters poll released on Wednesday forecast the crown would rise to 9.36 crowns per euro in three months from 9.44 now, and to 9.33 in six months.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARS\par \par \uc2 Unlike the Swiss and the Danes, \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a relaxed attitude towards its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last time \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in 2001, when it was trying to support the crown.\par \par \uc2 "We would not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market later on, but that is probably going to be the last resort," said Susanne Galler, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 Things have changed since 2001, though. At the height of the euro zone crisis in 2012, investors piled into the crown, thanks to Sweden's sound public finances and high credit ratings.\par \par \uc2 As the crisis ebbed and growth in Sweden weakened, the crown fell. But after the ECB announced its QE programme on Jan. 22, the crown reached a two-month high against the euro. The gains worsened the threat of deflation and bolstered the view that the Riksbank needs to act fast.\par \par \uc2 The crown's response to unconventional measures will be key to Riksbank efforts to lift inflation back towards its target of 2 percent from -0.3 percent now and whether it finally resorts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the crown does not depreciate significantly, then QE would probably provide only limited stimulus," Tina Mortensen, economist at Citi.\par \par \uc2 "And against the likely background of QE and/or rate cuts from the ECB, \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  and BOJ, a decision to start QE in Sweden may have relatively little effect on the exchange rate." (Reporting by Anirban Nag; Editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-5T12:42:14.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150205eb2500das\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
