\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: \uc2 H&M\uc2  3Q Spain in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jens Hansegard   \par \uc2 1,320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2012\par 05:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par 0755 Denmark Unemployment \par August \par 1030 Riksbank's Ekholm \par Speech \par 1100 Norges Bank Governor \par Olsen Speech \par  \par Other Macro \par 0900 EMU Consumer \par Confidence September \par 1230 US Durable Goods August \par 1400 Pending Home Sales August \par  \par Nordic Equities \par 0600 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q \par  \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Focus in the Nordics Thursday will be on third-quarter earnings from Swedish clothing retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HM-B.SK). While the giant fashion retailer published sales already two weeks ago, investors will be keen to know how the company's gross margin and bottom line have fared in the quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Analysts hope lower sourcing costs have raised the margin, but at the same time the company mainly sources in dollars and sells in euros; so the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  development in recent months is likely to have weighed on results. Investors will be interested to learn by just how much.\par \par \uc2 The general mood for European stocks soured Wednesday and the euro slid to a two week-low against the dollar, as worries about Spain and Greece and the ongoing discord between euro-zone leaders roiled markets. Spanish government bond yields pushed higher on increasing signs the latest euro-zone rescue plan was beginning to unravel.\par \par \uc2 On the macro front, Norges Bank Governor Olsen will give a speech Thursday. According to DNB the strong NOK will contribute towards \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  leaving its policy interest rate unchanged at 1.5% until October 2013. The bank also said that low international forward rates and recurrent waves of financial turmoil also signal an unchanged \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  rate for longer than previously expected.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT  7.3962-4040 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.3983-4059 \par %Chg -0.03 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT  8.4821-902 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.4807-77 \par %Chg  +0.02 \par  \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT  5.7437-88 \par Previous 2150 GMT 5.7483-535 \par %Chg -0.08 \par  \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT  6.5871-922 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.5894-937 \par %Chg  -0.03 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DNB analyst Kjersti Haugland said that the strong NOK will contribute towards \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  leaving its policy interest rate unchanged at 1.5% until October 2013. Low international forward rates and recurrent waves of financial turmoil also signal an unchanged \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate for longer than previously expected, Haugland says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecasts suggest the benchmark rate will be raised to 1.75% at some point between December 2012 and the summer of 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Haugland also notes that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's governor recently repeated his message that a krone that is too strong, that is one leading to too low inflation and too sluggish growth, will be met with interest rate cuts, not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Sweden\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Haugland expects the Riksbank to keep its benchmark repo rate at 1.25% until Spring 2014. Market pricing indicates investors expect a cut to 1% this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  1039.79   -20.33   -1.9% \par OMXS30 1088.16    -17.57   -1.6% \par OBX     415.43    -9.89    -2.3% \par OMXC20  494.04    -6.86    -1.4% \par OMXH25 2039.89    -51.43   -2.5% \par  \par Brent  $110.34    +0.34    +0.3% \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday Swedish clothing retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports full earnings for the third quarter at 0600 GMT. The Swedish retailer has already reported third quarter sales, which came in below expectations, due to a rainy summer and unusually warm weather in August in the company's main markets. But investors will closely watch how the company's gross margin has performed in the third quarter, and to what extent a weak euro and continued investments have offset lower sourcing costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Visibility is always low going into \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2  results due to the lack of detailed guidance," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from that, global events continue to determine sentiment and investors continue to fret over how the euro zone debt crisis will hit Nordic companies. In Greece, workers stage national strikes, and in Spain a call for early elections in the Catalonia region and anti-austerity street protests make Spain's situation look increasingly unstable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures were slightly higher in Asia trade, recovering some of Wednesday's falls on concerns about Europe, high oil stocks and weakening fuel demand. "From a broader based or macro perspective, we will continue to emphasize a disconnect between WTI crude oil and the equities as the petroleum complex continues to follow the euro lower," energy consulting firm Ritterbusch and Associates said. "We look for the euro zone to continue to be prioritized near term over any risk-on trade that might be implied by a steady stock market." It added that a weaker euro could push \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude below $90/bbl. It also noted that, for the rest of the week, a slew of U.S. economic data releases could support the stock market, but news about the bailout in Spain and Greece will be the main driver of oil prices. November \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude was up 38 cents at $90.36/bbl while Brent was up 34 cents at $110.38/bbl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norway's seasonally adjusted unemployment rate fell to 3.0% in July from 3.1% the previous month, data from Statistics Norway showed Wednesday. Unemployment in June was revised up to 3.1% from 3.0%, it said. Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast an unemployment rate of 3.1% in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ESTONIA: Ardo Hansson, the new president of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Estonia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said euro-zone governments may have relied too much on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fix the region's problems, urging cash-strapped countries to do their part by revising fiscal policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Maybe until now the governments have laid too big hopes on the role of the ECB, but I guess at the moment we all understand that everybody has to participate, especially those countries that are in trouble," Mr. Hansson said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Niels Bernstein, the Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor, said the Danish banking sector is still adapting to the impact of the financial crisis, but will come through it considerably strengthened. Mr. Bernstein said the Danish financial sector will continue to battle bad-loan problems for some time, but new regulations and higher firewalls will help the sector to withstand future crises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish consumer confidence dipped again in September in a sign that the debt crisis in Europe is weighing on sentiment even in the region's stronger economies. The closely watched consumer confidence indicator from the National Institute of Economic Research fell to 2.0 from 5.4 in August. This came as a surprise to economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires, who had forecast a rise to 5.7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Laval AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ALFA.SK), a Swedish maker of heat exchangers, separators, pumps and valves, said it secured an order of 50 million Swedish kronor ($7.62 million) to supply Alfa Laval Aalborg boilers to an offshore oil production vessel in Vietnam.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortum Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FUM1V.HE), a Finnish utility, said it has signed a co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  agreement with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7201.TO) for quick-charging stations in the Nordics. The aim is to create quick-charging infrastructure for electric vehicles to make them a viable option for all car drivers, it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saab AB (SAAB-B.SK)\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a Swedish defense company, said it has received a $31 million order from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. army\f28 \uc2 \f2  for the company's Carl-Gustaf man-portable weapon system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lemminkainen Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LEM1S.HE), a Finnish construction company, said the Finnish competition authorities have approved the sale of its concrete business to Rudus without conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Jens Hansegard at jens.hansegard@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Charles Duxbury in Stockholm contributed to this report.) [ 09-26-12 2315ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 77791   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120927e89r0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi