\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Swedish June trade surplus hits all-time highs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Starck \par \uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2001\par 14:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  STOCKHOLM, July 27 (Reuters) - Sweden's trade surplus rose to 15.4 billion crowns ($1.45 billion) in June - the largest surplus on record for any month since the launch in 1975 of the current method of collecting the data. \par \par \uc2  "It's the biggest figure ever," an official at Statistics Sweden (SCB), the national statistics office, told Reuters on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Exports rose to 66.0 billion crowns from 65.8 billion a year earlier, but were below May's 67.2 billion. Imports fell to 50.6 billion crowns, the lowest monthly figure since July 2000, from 53.3 billion and 54.8 billion respectively. \par \par \uc2  Swedish trade with fellow European Union countries generated a surplus of 1.9 billion crowns in June while trade with the rest of the world contributed 13.5 billion crowns. \par \par \uc2  The 0.3 percent year-on-year rise in exports defied analyst's expectations that they are set on a downward trend because of the slowdown in the world economy. \par \par \uc2  In June 2000, Sweden's economy was expanding at 4.6 percent, or double the pace in Q1 this year. \par \par \uc2  "We had expected a fall in exports...The weak crown can be an explanation why exports are going strong although demand is falling globally," said Johan Javeus, an analyst at Handelsbanken. \par \par \uc2  CROWN DOWN SIX PCT THIS YEAR \par \par \uc2  Measured by a trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket index , the Swedish crown has fallen six percent this year and 15 percent in the past 12 months. The crown has dropped almost 25 percent against the globally firmer U.S. dollar . \par \par \uc2  The crown's slide prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Riksbank, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by purchasing crowns in an effort to turn the weakening trend, which it said threatened its two-percent inflation target. \par \par \uc2  On July 5, the Riksbank raised key interest rates by a quarter point to 4.25 percent in a move it said was aimed at curbing inflationary pressures caused by the weaker crown. \par \par \uc2  A depreciating crown makes Swedish goods relatively cheaper to buy on international markets while importers in Sweden must spend more crowns on their purchases of goods from abroad. \par \par \uc2  In the first half of 2001 Swedish exports rose one percent year-on-year while imports grew three percent. The trade surplus dipped to 64.0 billion crowns from 69.6 billion in January-June 2000, the SCB said. \par \par \uc2  The National Institute of Economic Research (KI), an influential Swedish think-tank, said on Thursday industrial activity had weakened further in April-June with firms reporting a decline in order inflow from domestic and export markets. \par \par \uc2  "Export prices are estimated to drop slightly," the KI said, citing expectations among the companies it had surveyed. \par \par \uc2  Also on Thursday, the SCB said Swedish export prices had risen 2.2 percent year-on-year in June compared with a 6.9 percent advance in import prices. (Additional reporting by Anders Hultman in Stockholm). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010727dx7r00nxx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
