\par\par \b \uc2  Swedish c.bank official-weak crown effecting inflation. \b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2001\par 09:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  STOCKHOLM, June 15 (Reuters) - The long term weakness in the Swedish crown was having an impact on inflation which could prompt direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Deputy Governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or Riksbank, Lars Heikensten said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The crown had shifted its "risk spectrum," Heikensten said in a speech in the southern Swedish town of Malmo. \par \par \uc2  Heikensten said the Riksbank had presented an inflation report on May 31 whose chief message was that in the period one to two years ahead - the main focus of monetary policy - inflation would be in line with the bank's two percent target. \par \par \uc2  "For a number of reasons, however, the picture was more uncertain than usual," Heikensten said. \par \par \uc2  Inflation figures presented on Thursday were in line with the Riksbank's assesment in May, Heikensten said. \par \par \uc2  "There is however one clear deviation from what we counted on. The Swedish crown has continued to weaken, which is the opposite of the assumptions on which the Inflation Report's main scenario is based." \par \par \uc2  Heikensten said that amounted to a shift in the spectrum of inflation risks. \par \par \uc2  If the exchange rate were to remain weak it could have consequences for monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "... in a situtation where the exchange rate deviates from a reasonable value and simultaneously constitutes a risk for inflation, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be motivated," Heikensten added. \par \par \uc2  Statistics \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday released headline CPI numbers for May which were up 0.7 percent compared with April and up 3.0 percent year-on-year - higher than analysts had expected. \par \par \uc2  According to a Reuters poll, analysts had expected a 0.5 percent increase month-on-month and a 2.8 percent rise year-on-year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6f01kw3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
