\par\par \b \uc2  Market ructions seen limited as Danish vote nears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Saft \par \uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2000\par 14:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Financial markets will feel repurcussions from Thursday's closely-run Danish vote on euro membership, but the threat of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro is likely to limit any fallout. \par \par \uc2  With just one day left before Thursday's referendum, the race was still too close to call, though the majority of polls were predicting a "no" by the Danes. \par \par \uc2  A rejection risks being interpreted as a vote of no-confidence in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  project, making it a negative for the euro and euro zone assets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But any attempts to bid down the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be made with great trepidation after last Friday's coordinated Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, the first such move since 1995. \par \par \uc2  "In the event of a "no" vote there will be some downside pressure on euro/dollar but the market will tread very, very carfully," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at around $0.8850 on Wednesday, near the top of its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading range. \par \par \uc2  Traders have had a long time to get used to the idea of a rejection by the Danes, which in itself should limit their reaction, Piron said. \par \par \uc2  "I am looking for only a modest move, this has been talked to death by the markets." \par \par \uc2  The euro's performance, in turn, is the linchpin for any reaction on euro zone bond and stock markets, where a steep fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would prompt sympathetic downward moves. \par \par \uc2  Also hanging fire on the results are Denmark's own markets, which will be most directly affected, and bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from euro candidates Britain and \b \highlight22\uc2 Sweden\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SAFETY NET FOR EURO \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts have cited signs that Danish opposition to entry was hardening in recent weeks as one of the factors which assisted the euro's slide to record lows last week. \par \par \uc2  But all bets of how far the euro would fall if the Danes rejected euro entry are off after the Group of Seven industrial nations \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support last week. \par \par \uc2  Concern the G7 will mount another joint euro-buying spree if the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under renewed pressure is expected to restrain euro bears' enthusiasm in the event of a rejection. \par \par \uc2  "If it is a no vote, would you sell euros if the ECB was going to be just around the corner?" said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns International in London. \par \par \uc2  "Last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes the vote more difficult to trade off." \par \par \uc2  Likewise, a "yes" vote is seen as having limited upside for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euro zone bonds. While Denmark is seen as generally fiscally solid, it represents only about two percent of the euro zone economic whole. In addition, Denmark is seen as likely foot-dragger on structural reform. \par \par \uc2  On bond markets, analysts held out the potential that a "no" vote would prompt flight to safety in core euro issuers such as Germany and France, with underperformance from Italy. But this will most likely be driven by euro falls, with a sharp independent move by the bond market seen as unlikely. \par \par \uc2  NO DRAMA FOR EURO OUTS \par \par \uc2  The outcome of the Danish referendum with the vote is seen swaying public opinion on euro entry in Britain and had, in recent months, been expected to influence the pound given any British euro entry is expected to occur at a weaker exchange rate than sterling is currently trading at. \par \par \uc2  But the pound has already become much less sensitive to the referendum as Britain's euro-friendly Labour government flagging political fortunes are seen making British entry into the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a more distant prospect. \par \par \uc2  The Swedish public is also expected to be influenced by the Danish vote but the Swedish crown is seen less liable to move violently in its wake. \par \par \uc2  DANISH ASSETS SEEN VULNERABLE BUT NO 1992 \par \par \uc2  If "no" carries the day the sharpest moves may well be Danish markets. \par \par \uc2  Credit Lyonnais is expecting a no vote to drive Danish 10-year bond spreads over bunds to about 60 basis points from its current 41, as investors factor in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk and kiss convergence plays goodbye. \par \par \uc2  On the flip side, a "yes" will fuel a rally taking spreads eventually down to about 25 basis points, according to Credit Lyonnais. \par \par \uc2  The Danish crown will face selling pressure in the event of a rejection. \par \par \uc2  "In the case of a no the (Danish) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to prove once again that it means business," Unibank senior economist Anne Buchardt told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "They will probably have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I guess they will have to raise rates as well." \par \par \uc2  Danish authorities have pledged to maintain the crown's fixed exchange rate link with the euro even if Danes reject the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to "do everything in its power" to keep Denmark in Exchange Rate Mechanism-2. \par \par \uc2  Denmark's shock rejection of the Maastrict treaty in June 1992 cast a pall of uncertainty over Europe's push towards a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and undermined the European exchange rate mechanism (ERM). \par \par \uc2  This culminated in sterling and the Italian lira's ejection from the ERM in September, forcing the French to call a similar referendum. \par \par \uc2  But with the euro now a reality the project is far more resilient, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Swaha Pattanaik in London). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9r06k2s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
